- **Breaking change:** `fleetctl gitops` now resets every setting omitted from `org_settings` and fleet `settings` to its default, including `gitops`, `host_expiry_settings`, and `smtp_settings`. If your YAML doesn't include `smtp_settings`, GitOps clears the SMTP settings and password stored in Fleet. Fleet then stops sending invite, password reset, and multi-factor authentication (MFA) emails, so users who log in with MFA can't log in. Add these settings to your YAML before you run GitOps with Fleet 5. `fleetctl gitops` warns before resetting `smtp_settings`, `gitops`, or `server_settings` values.
- **Breaking change:** `PATCH /api/v1/fleet/config` and `fleetctl gitops` now require `smtp_settings.password` when changing the SMTP `server`, `port`, `user_name`, or `authentication_type`, so the stored password is never sent to a different SMTP server. A blank `smtp_settings.password` keeps the password stored in Fleet when those settings don't change.
- Added `gitops` and `smtp_settings` to `fleetctl generate-gitops` output.
- Changed `GET /api/v1/fleet/config` to return `smtp_settings` (with the password masked) to users with the global GitOps role.
